\ Dogfood: Phase 4 slice 12 (declared combinator recognition). A word taking an
\ ordinary `[ ... ]` parameter is a *real call*, not a splice: `apply` mints its
\ own symbol and receives its argument as a runtime `(code, env)` quotation
\ value. `apply2` forwards that value down a second call level, and `run` calls
\ one and returns its result. A word that must be spliced instead declares
\ `inline` over a `~[ ... ]` parameter (see `lib/core/combinators.sth`).

import: intrinsics * ;

: apply ( [ i64 -- i64 ] i64 -- i64 ) | n | | f | n f call ;

: apply2 ( [ i64 -- i64 ] i64 -- i64 ) apply ;

: run ( [ -- i64 ] -- i64 ) call ;

: main ( -- )
  [ 1 add ] 5 apply .
  [ 2 mul ] 20 apply2 .
  [ 42 ] run . ;
